#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093495 :tutorial/content "An **indicator function** is a function that maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset.\n\nThe indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A \\subseteq X$ is the function\n\n![](images/af/af268dd04b20dde1e7216e63839f309f572615b59f57f81515cd2e7998c1f45d.svg)\n\nFor example, let $A = \\{ a,c \\}$ be a subset of the universal set $X=\\{ a,b,c,d,e \\}.$ Then, the indicator function $\\mathbf{1}_A(x)$ of $A$ is the following:\n\n![](images/7b/7b1ec0a80d5ec7dc9974d2ebaf6984e94faa00d73ec624dc48d56addbe701ee1.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the elements $a$ and $c$ (the elements of our subset) are mapped to $1,$ and all other elements are mapped to $0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "20986866-6719-520f-b707-cf6b26e3823b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8006 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093496 :tutorial/content "Recall that the indicator function of a set $A \\subseteq X$ is the function\n\n![](images/74/7408ad6ce2700ac59d8e6a78c511ba2b51551e6b9d039ee7e2f8548f4177802e.svg)\n\nTherefore, the indicator function of the complement $\\overline{A}$ is given by \n\n![](images/de/de1a51c00e16d836fde350e0936fb9cb63124c72b49174210be3e6f6faa152da.svg)\n\nThis means that the indicator function of $A$ and its complement are related as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the indicator functions of $A \\cap B$ and union $A \\cup B$ can be expressed through the indicator functions of $A$ and $B.$\n\n- For the indicator function of the *intersection*, we have: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)} \\\\  & ={1}_{A}(x)\\cdot {1}_{B}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the indicator function of the *union*, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)} \\\\  & ={1}_{A}(x)+{1}_{B}(x)-{1}_{A}(x)\\cdot {1}_{B}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can prove these results by splitting them into cases." :tutorial/id #uuid "a111c445-39e3-5ddd-8272-48ef3795a3b0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8007 :tutorial/title "Properties of Indicator Functions"}]}
